dp-grpc — Release Artifacts and Verification
===========================================

This repository publishes versioned dp-grpc Java artifacts via GitHub Releases.
Each release corresponds to a Git tag of the form:

  rel-<version>

Example:
  rel-1.12.0-rc8

------------------------------------------------------------
Release Contents
------------------------------------------------------------

Each release contains:

  dp-grpc-<version>.jar
    Compiled Java gRPC interfaces and generated classes.

  dp-grpc-<version>.tar.gz
    The .proto source files, for generating stubs in other languages.

  SHA256SUMS
    SHA-256 checksums for the files above, in sha256sum format.

  SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle
    A keyless Sigstore signature over SHA256SUMS.

Older releases instead shipped a separate dp-grpc-<version>.jar.sha256 and
dp-grpc-<version>.tar.gz.sha256, and were not signed.  A release page showing
those two files rather than SHA256SUMS predates signing; the verification
steps below do not apply to it.

------------------------------------------------------------
Download and Installation
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Download the artifacts from the GitHub Release page into a single directory,
   with no subdirectories.  Both commands below expect to find the files beside
   SHA256SUMS.

2. Verify the checksums:

     sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS

   The output should indicate:

     dp-grpc-<version>.jar: OK
     dp-grpc-<version>.tar.gz: OK

   SHA256SUMS lists the two downloadable artifacts shown above, so this fails if
   you downloaded only one of them -- most consumers want just the JAR.  To check
   the files you actually have and ignore the rest:

     sha256sum --ignore-missing -c SHA256SUMS

   Note that --ignore-missing succeeds if it checked nothing at all, so confirm
   the file you care about is listed as OK rather than relying on the exit code.

   SHA256SUMS does not list itself or SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle, so this step says
   nothing about either file.  The signature check in step 3 is what covers them:
   it verifies the bundle against SHA256SUMS, and these checksums in turn cover
   the artifacts.

3. Verify the signature.

   The checksums establish integrity but not origin: they are published to the
   same release page as the artifacts, so anyone able to replace a file could
   replace its checksum alongside it.  The Sigstore signature closes that gap by
   binding SHA256SUMS to the repository, workflow file, tag, and source commit
   that produced it.  There is no public key to fetch or trust: the signing
   identity is a short-lived certificate issued to the GitHub Actions run and
   recorded in the public Rekor transparency log.

   Install cosign:

     https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/system_config/installation/

   Then:

     cosign verify-blob \
       --bundle SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle \
       --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-grpc/\.github/workflows/release\.yml@refs/tags/rel-' \
       --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
       SHA256SUMS

   The output should be:

     Verified OK

   Keep the --certificate-identity-regexp exactly as written.  It is anchored at
   the start and pinned to this repository, this workflow file, and a rel- tag.
   A loosened or unanchored pattern would accept a valid Sigstore signature made
   by any workflow in any repository, which is the usual way this check is made
   vacuous while still appearing to pass.

4. Use the JAR as a Maven dependency or include it directly on the classpath.

------------------------------------------------------------
Notes
------------------------------------------------------------

- dp-grpc contains only API and generated code.
- It does not run as a standalone service.
- Runtime behavior depends on consumers such as dp-service and dp-desktop-app.
